From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Divy Srivastava <divy@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:35:20 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [snapshot] Share kept bytecode between the startup and
 context snapshots

With FunctionCodeHandling::kKeep, a SharedFunctionInfo keeps its
bytecode, and the startup serializer serializes it along with the
SharedFunctionInfo. The context can reach the same objects in two ways:
- An error's captured stack frames hold the BytecodeWrapper of each
  interpreted frame, and through it the bytecode, its constant pool and
  everything in that (literal boilerplates, BigInts).
- A BigInt literal in a constant pool can also be the value of a context
  variable.
The context serializer then serialized a second copy. Debug builds abort
("!startup_serializer_->ReferenceMapContains(obj)"); release builds
silently write the duplicates into the context snapshot.

Send BytecodeWrapper, BytecodeArray and BigInt through the startup
object cache, as for SharedFunctionInfos, ScopeInfos and HeapNumbers.
The bytecode is the SharedFunctionInfo's own, which it already shares;
only its lazily collected source positions change after compilation.
BigInts are immutable. With kClear, the SharedFunctionInfos drop their
bytecode first, so an error's frames are the only holder and their
bytecode moves from the context snapshot to the startup snapshot.
---
 src/snapshot/context-serializer.cc | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/snapshot/context-serializer.cc b/src/snapshot/context-serializer.cc
index 3c2605a3588..9a472422238 100644
--- a/src/snapshot/context-serializer.cc
+++ b/src/snapshot/context-serializer.cc
@@ -251,8 +251,16 @@ bool ContextSerializer::ShouldBeInTheStartupObjectCache(Tagged<HeapObject> o) {
   // We can't allow scripts to be part of the context snapshot because they
   // contain a unique ID, and deserializing several context snapshots containing
   // script would cause dupes.
+  //
+  // With FunctionCodeHandling::kKeep, a SharedFunctionInfo keeps its bytecode,
+  // and the context can reach the same bytecode through an error's captured
+  // stack frames, which hold the bytecode of interpreted frames. Sharing it is
+  // what the SharedFunctionInfo does anyway; only its lazily collected source
+  // positions change after compilation. A BigInt literal in the constant pool
+  // can also be the value of a context variable; BigInts are immutable.
   return IsName(o) || IsScript(o) || IsSharedFunctionInfo(o) ||
-         IsHeapNumber(o) || IsCode(o) || IsInstructionStream(o) ||
+         IsBytecodeArray(o) || IsBytecodeWrapper(o) || IsHeapNumber(o) ||
+         IsBigInt(o) || IsCode(o) || IsInstructionStream(o) ||
          IsScopeInfo(o) || IsAccessorInfo(o) || IsInterceptorInfo(o) ||
          IsTemplateInfo(o) || IsClassPositions(o) ||
          o->map() == ReadOnlyRoots(isolate()).fixed_cow_array_map();
